---
/**
 * Terms of use (the website) and of service (the extension and its licences).
 *
 * Written to be read, not to be impressive. Anything a school's procurement
 * will actually ask about — what happens if it breaks, who owns the data,
 * whether we can withdraw it — is answered plainly rather than buried.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import {
  legal,
  licenceSync,
  ownership,
  pricing,
  refunds,
  toddleNotice,
  updatedLabel,
} from "../../data/legal";
import { toddleExtension } from "../../data/site";

const title = "Terms of use and service — Nyuchi Learning";
const description =
  "The terms of use for learning.nyuchi.com and the terms of service for the Toddle Enhancement Extension: what a licence grants, how keys work, refunds, acceptable use, liability and governing law.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/legal/terms">
  <article class="container-prose section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-h1 text-foreground text-balance">
      Terms of use and service
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Last updated {updatedLabel}. Between you and {legal.entity}, operating as
      {legal.operator}. The terms of use cover this website; the terms of service cover
      the {toddleExtension.name} and its licences.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 ubuntu-quote">
      <p class="text-body text-pretty">
        <strong>The short version.</strong> Use it in your school. The extension and its
        code are ours, and a licence lets you use them, not own them. Do not resell it,
        copy its code into anything else, or take it apart to make a competing product.
        Reading the code to check what it does is welcome. It reads Toddle and changes nothing, but
        it is software that rides on someone else's app, so it can break — check anything
        that matters before you act on it. We are not liable for what you do with what it
        shows you.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Using this website</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      You may read, link to and quote this website freely. Please do not try to break it,
      overload it, or use it to attack anyone else; security research is welcome under our
      <a href="/legal/vulnerability-disclosure" class="link">vulnerability disclosure
      policy</a>. We keep it accurate, but it describes software that changes, so where a
      decision matters, check with us. Links to other sites are there to help; we are not
      responsible for what those sites say or do. How the site uses cookies and data is in
      the <a href="/legal/cookies" class="link">cookie policy</a> and the
      <a href="/legal/privacy" class="link">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ownership" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      Who owns the extension
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      {ownership.statement} That covers its code, its design and its documentation, in
      every version, including the free features. {ownership.licence}
      {ownership.noReuse} {ownership.reading}
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">What a licence lets you do</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Install and use the {toddleExtension.name} for teaching and administration at the
      school or organisation the licence covers, on as many machines as that licence
      allows, for as long as it is current. The free features may be used by anyone, on
      the same terms. Using it gives you no other rights in it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">What it costs</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Concealing student flags for a shared screen is free and is not licensed. It
      protects a child, so it is not something we will ever put behind a payment. The My classes filter on the Toddle
      home page is free too. A licence covers the gradebook's per-criterion columns, the
      CSV export, the student sidebar, primary teacher names and the Attendance
      dashboard's student details.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      {
        pricing.map((plan) => (
          <li>
            <strong class="text-foreground">
              {plan.name} — {plan.price} {plan.period}.
            </strong>{" "}
            {plan.who}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      A licence runs for twelve months from the day it is issued and does not renew
      itself — nothing is charged to you automatically, and a key simply stops working
      when its year is up. Payment is taken by
      <a href="https://buymeacoffee.com" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link"
        >Buy Me a Coffee</a
      >, which processes cards through Stripe; we never hold your card details. Prices are
      in US dollars and exclude any tax your own country adds.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      An organisation licence is one key for the school's email domain, sent to the contact
      who signed up. Anyone signed in to Toddle with an address at that domain is covered.
      The number of teachers you give when you buy helps us plan support; it does not
      limit who the key works for.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Refunds</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">{refunds.rule}</strong> Ask by emailing{" "}
      <a href={`mailto:${legal.supportEmail}`} class="link">{legal.supportEmail}</a> from
      the address you bought with, or through Buy Me a Coffee. {refunds.key}
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">How licence keys work</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      A licence key is a signed statement that the extension checks on your own device, so
      activating it contacts nothing and works offline. A key carries the buyer's email
      address, and may also name who it is for: the buyer's email, for the Toddle account
      with that address, or for an organisation licence the school's email domain, for
      Toddle accounts on that domain. Where it does, the comparison happens in your browser
      only. {licenceSync.short} While a key is entered, the extension downloads at most once a day a signed list of
      cancelled keys, sending nothing about you; a key we have cancelled stops unlocking the
      licensed features once the extension has seen that list. If the check cannot be made,
      the last result stands, so a network outage never switches a licence off. When a key
      expires the licensed features stop, and the free flag switch and My classes filter
      carry on working.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">What you may not do</h2>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>Resell it, sublicense it, or charge anyone else for access to it.</li>
      <li>
        Copy, modify, reuse or redistribute its code, in whole or in part, in another
        product or service, without our written permission. That includes publishing it,
        or a changed copy of it, anywhere else.
      </li>
      <li>
        Take it apart in order to build a competing product. Reading the code to satisfy
        yourself about what it does is fine — we encourage it.
      </li>
      <li>Share a licence key beyond the organisation it was issued to.</li>
      <li>Remove or obscure the attribution in the interface.</li>
      <li>
        Try to get round the licence check, or to make the extension read, send or change
        data it is built not to.
      </li>
      <li>
        Use it in a way that breaks your school's own policies, or Toddle's terms for your
        school's account.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Your data stays yours</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      We claim no ownership or licence over anything the extension reads. It transmits
      nothing it reads to us, so there is nothing for us to claim. Outside Toddle it makes
      only two connections: feedback you write yourself and choose to send from its menu,
      and, while a licence is entered, a daily check for cancelled keys that sends nothing
      about you. Its
      message buttons only open Toddle's own chat; any message is sent by you, through
      Toddle. The <a href="/legal/privacy" class="link">privacy policy</a> sets out the
      detail, and the <a href="/legal/security" class="link">security page</a> how it is
      enforced and tested, and how to report a vulnerability.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">It rides on Toddle</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      This matters more than the usual boilerplate, so it is said plainly. The extension
      works by reading the interface Toddle's own web app uses. Toddle does not publish
      that interface and is under no obligation to keep it stable. A Toddle release can
      change it, and when that happens a column may stop appearing or a flag may stop being
      concealed.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      We will fix breakages as quickly as we can, and the extension is built to fail
      visibly rather than quietly. But we cannot promise it will always work, because that
      is not ours to promise. Treat anything you are about to act on — a report, a
      moderation decision, a conversation with a parent — as something to confirm in Toddle
      itself.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      {toddleNotice} "Toddle" is used here only to say what this software works with.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Liability</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      The software is provided as it is. To the extent the law allows, we are not liable
      for indirect or consequential loss, and our total liability is limited to what you
      paid us in the twelve months before the claim. Nothing here limits liability for
      death or personal injury caused by negligence, for fraud, or for anything else the
      law does not permit us to limit.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Ending it</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      You can stop using it at any time by removing the extension. We can end a licence if
      these terms are breached. If we withdraw the product altogether we will say so with
      as much notice as we can manage, and refund the unused part of a current licence.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Changes</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If these terms change, the date at the top changes and the current version lives
      here. A change that materially reduces what you are entitled to will be notified to
      the contact on the licence, not slipped in.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Law</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      These terms are governed by the law of {legal.country}, and the courts of
      {legal.city} have jurisdiction over any dispute about them. If your institution needs a
      different governing law or its own contract, write to
      <a href={`mailto:${legal.supportEmail}`} class="link">{legal.supportEmail}</a> — that
      is a normal request and we would rather have the conversation than have you sign
      something that does not work for you.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">The other legal pages</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      These terms sit alongside the
      <a href="/legal/privacy" class="link">privacy policy</a>,
      <a href="/legal/cookies" class="link">cookie policy</a>,
      <a href="/legal/data" class="link">data handling</a>,
      <a href="/legal/student-privacy" class="link">student privacy</a>,
      <a href="/legal/security" class="link">security</a> and
      <a href="/legal/notice" class="link">legal notice</a> pages.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
